/** @page http_client_migration Migrating to HttpClientIo and RestClientIo

    @tableofcontents

    @section http_client_migration_overview Overview

    The @ref httpclientiointro "HttpClientIo" and @ref restclientiointro "RestClientIo" modules are the
    asynchronous successors of the built-in @ref Qore::HTTPClient "HTTPClient" class and of the
    @ref restclientintro "RestClient" module.  They speak HTTP/1.1, HTTP/2, and HTTP/3 behind one API,
    multiplex concurrent requests over shared connections, and perform all I/O on the process-wide
    asynchronous I/O controller, so that many requests can be in progress without a thread for each one.

    This page lists what corresponds to what when an application moves from the synchronous clients to the
    asynchronous ones, and where the new modules deliberately behave differently.  The new modules do not copy
    every detail of the old API: where the old behavior was a historical accident (for example a timeout that
    limits each read instead of the request, or a GET request body that is sent without comment), the new
    modules keep their own semantics, and this page describes how to get the result the application needs.

    |!Synchronous API|!Asynchronous API|!Notes
    |@ref Qore::HTTPClient "HTTPClient"|@ref HttpClientIo::HttpClientConnectionManager "HttpClientConnectionManager"|one manager serves any number of origins; the URL is given with each request
    |\c HTTPClient::startPollSendRecv()|@ref HttpClientIo::HttpClientRequestPollOperation "HttpClientRequestPollOperation"|a request for poll loops and async I/O controllers; \c ConnectionProvider::HttpClientIoPollOperation keeps the output format of the old poll operation
    |@ref restclientintro "RestClient"|@ref restclientiointro "RestClientIo"|the same data serialization and schema validation; the OAuth2 client credentials and password grants
    |\c RestClient::RestConnection::get()|\c RestClient::RestConnection::getAsync()|returns a \c RestClientIo object
    |\c ConnectionProvider::HttpConnection::get()|\c ConnectionProvider::HttpConnection::getAsync()|returns a connection manager

    @section http_client_migration_requests Making Requests

    A connection manager is created once and shared; each request names its URL.  Unlike
    @ref Qore::HTTPClient "HTTPClient", the manager never raises an exception for an HTTP error status: the
    response is returned, and the caller checks \c status_code.

    @code{.py}
%requires HttpClientIo

HttpClientConnectionManager mgr(<HttpClientConnectionManagerOptions>{
    "connect_timeout": 10s,
    "request_timeout": 60s,
});
hash<auto> resp = mgr.request("https://api.example.com", "GET", "/orders/42");
if (resp.status_code != 200) {
    throw "ORDER-ERROR", sprintf("HTTP %d: %s", resp.status_code, resp.status_message);
}
    @endcode

    |!HTTPClient|!HttpClientConnectionManager
    |\c send(), \c get(), \c head(), \c post()|@ref HttpClientIo::HttpClientConnectionManager::request() "request()": the response hash has \c status_code, \c status_message, \c headers (lower-case names), \c body, \c effective_url, and \c redirects
    |the \c info reference argument|the response hash: \c effective_url, \c redirects, \c protocol, \c headers_raw
    |\c sendWithRecvCallback(), \c sendAndStream(), receiving into an output stream|@ref HttpClientIo::HttpClientConnectionManager::openStream() "openStream()" and @ref HttpClientIo::HttpClientResponseStream::read() "HttpClientResponseStream::read()"
    |\c sendWithSendCallback(), \c sendChunked()|@ref HttpClientIo::HttpClientConnectionManager::acquireAndStartStreaming() "acquireAndStartStreaming()" with request-body streaming and @ref HttpClientIo::HttpClientStreamHandle::sendData() "HttpClientStreamHandle::sendData()"; see @ref http_client_migration_trailers for trailers
    |\c startPollSendRecv() and the other poll APIs|@ref HttpClientIo::HttpClientRequestPollOperation "HttpClientRequestPollOperation", or @ref HttpClientIo::HttpClientConnectionManager::requestAsync() "requestAsync()", which returns a cancellable Future
    |\c setUserPassword()|the \c credentials request option (@ref HttpClientIo::HttpClientRequestOptions "HttpClientRequestOptions") or user information in the URL
    |\c setURL(), \c setProxyURL(), \c setTimeout(), and other setters|options given when the manager is created; a manager is not reconfigured while it is in use

    A text response body is decoded to a string in its character encoding by the rules shared by all HTTP
    clients (see @ref Qore::get_http_body_encoding() "get_http_body_encoding()"); a request can receive the
    octets of the body instead (the \c decode_text argument).

    @section http_client_migration_options Options

    |!HTTPClient / RestClient option|!HttpClientIo / RestClientIo option|!Notes
    |\c http_version|\c protocol: \c "auto", \c "h1", \c "h2", or \c "h3"|\c RestClientIo also accepts \c http_version; \c "1.0" and \c "1.1" select HTTP/1.1
    |\c http3_mode \c "disabled"|\c enable_http3 = @ref False|HTTP/3 is not used in \c "auto" mode
    |\c http3_mode \c "required"|\c protocol \c "h3"|only HTTP/3 is used
    |\c timeout|\c request_timeout (manager) or \c timeout (\c RestClientIo)|see @ref http_client_migration_timeouts
    |\c connect_timeout|\c connect_timeout|the same
    |\c max_redirects, \c redirect_passthru|\c max_redirects, \c redirect_passthru|the same rules; see @ref http_client_migration_redirects
    |\c ssl_verify_cert|\c ssl_verify_mode (\c RestClientIo also accepts \c ssl_verify_cert)|the certificate of an HTTPS proxy is verified with the same options
    |\c ssl_ca_file, \c ssl_ca_path|\c ssl_ca_file, \c ssl_ca_path|the same
    |\c ssl_cert_path, \c ssl_cert_data, \c ssl_key_path, \c ssl_key_data, \c ssl_key_password|\c client_cert, \c client_pk (manager); the same options in \c RestClientIo|the client certificate is sent to the server, never to a proxy
    |\c proxy|\c proxy_url (manager) or \c proxy (\c RestClientIo)|see @ref http_client_migration_proxies
    |\c assume_encoding|\c assume_encoding|the same
    |\c encoding|\c encoding (\c RestClientIo)|the character encoding of string request bodies
    |\c encoding_passthru|\c encoding_passthru|the same
    |\c max_response_body_size|\c max_response_body_size|also a request option; see @ref HttpClientIo::HttpClientRequestOptions "HttpClientRequestOptions"
    |\c headers|\c headers (\c RestClientIo)|the manager sends \c User-Agent and \c Accept-Encoding by default
    |\c username, \c password|\c credentials request option (manager); \c username, \c password (\c RestClientIo)|see @ref http_client_migration_auth
    |\c pre_encoded_urls, \c encode_chars|\c pre_encoded_urls, \c encode_chars (\c RestClientIo); @ref HttpClientIo::encode_http_request_target() "encode_http_request_target()"|the same encoding rules
    |\c default_path|\c default_path (\c RestClientIo)|the same
    |\c protocols, \c default_port|none|a URL gives its scheme and port; a URL without a scheme is an \c https URL, and other schemes than \c http and \c https raise \c HTTP-CLIENT-UNKNOWN-PROTOCOL
    |\c additional_methods|none|every request method is allowed

    Options that \c RestClientIo does not know are ignored, so that subclasses and connections can pass options
    of their own in the same hash.

    @section http_client_migration_timeouts Timeouts

    The \c timeout of @ref Qore::HTTPClient "HTTPClient" limits each read and write on the socket, so a slow
    download that keeps receiving data never times out.  The \c request_timeout option of
    @ref HttpClientIo::HttpClientConnectionManagerOptions "HttpClientConnectionManagerOptions" (the \c timeout
    option of \c RestClientIo) is instead the deadline for the whole response, including its body.

    |!Need|!Configuration
    |a bounded response time (APIs)|\c request_timeout, as by default (60 seconds)
    |a long transfer that must keep making progress|\c request_timeout 0 (no deadline) and \c io_timeout, the maximum time without receiving data
    |both|\c request_timeout and \c io_timeout

    The \c timeout option of \c RestClientIo sets a deadline; 0 or no value keeps the manager's.  A
    \c RestClientIo object without a deadline is created with a shared connection manager whose
    \c request_timeout is 0, given as the second argument of the constructor.  \c RestClientIo also has an
    \c io_timeout option.

    A timeout raises \c HTTPCLIENT-TIMEOUT instead of \c SOCKET-TIMEOUT.

    @section http_client_migration_redirects Redirects

    All request APIs that return a whole response follow redirects with the rules of
    @ref HttpUtil::HttpRedirectFollower "HttpRedirectFollower", which are the rules of
    @ref Qore::HTTPClient "HTTPClient": 301, 302, 303, 307, and 308 are followed; a 303 (except for a \c HEAD
    request) and a \c POST redirected with 301 or 302 are retrieved with \c GET; the \c Authorization, \c Cookie,
    and \c Host headers are sent only to the origin of the original request.  This includes the asynchronous and
    poll APIs of \c RestClientIo, its SSE APIs,
    @ref HttpClientIo::HttpClientConnectionManager::requestAsync() "requestAsync()",
    @ref HttpClientIo::HttpClientRequestPollOperation "HttpClientRequestPollOperation", and
    @ref HttpClientIo::HttpClientConnectionManager::openStream() "openStream()".  The lower-level stream APIs
    (@ref HttpClientIo::HttpClientConnectionManager::acquireAndStartStreaming() "acquireAndStartStreaming()",
    @ref HttpClientIo::HttpClientConnectionManager::submitRequestAsync() "submitRequestAsync()", and the methods
    of @ref HttpClientIo::HttpClientStreamHandle "HttpClientStreamHandle") send a single request.

    The response has the final URL in \c effective_url and the redirects followed in \c redirects; the errors
    are those of @ref Qore::HTTPClient "HTTPClient": \c HTTP-CLIENT-REDIRECT-ERROR,
    \c HTTP-CLIENT-MAXIMUM-REDIRECTS-EXCEEDED, and \c HTTP-CLIENT-UNKNOWN-PROTOCOL.

    @section http_client_migration_auth Authentication

    Credentials are given with each request, or as user information in the request URL, and are sent only to
    the origin of the request.  They are sent with the Basic scheme; a Digest challenge (RFC 7616) is answered,
    and later requests to the origin are authenticated with Digest without another challenge.  The credentials
    in the proxy URL answer the challenges of the proxy, also for \c CONNECT requests.  See
    @ref HttpUtil::HttpAuthenticator "HttpAuthenticator".

    @code{.py}
hash<auto> resp = mgr.request("https://files.example.com", "GET", "/reports/q3.pdf", NOTHING, NOTHING,
    NOTHING, False, <HttpClientRequestOptions>{
        "credentials": <HttpClientCredentials>{"username": "reports", "password": pwd},
    });
    @endcode

    \c RestClientIo sends its \c username and \c password the same way, with every request API, unless an
    OAuth2 grant type is set.

    @section http_client_migration_cookies Cookies

    @ref Qore::HTTPClient "HTTPClient" does not keep cookies.  A connection manager has a cookie jar that stores
    the cookies of responses and sends them with requests (RFC 6265), unless \c enable_cookies is @ref False.
    A request can use a cookie jar of its own, or no cookies (see
    @ref HttpClientIo::HttpClientRequestOptions "HttpClientRequestOptions").  A \c RestClientIo object created
    with a shared connection manager has a cookie jar of its own, so that clients sharing a manager do not share
    cookies.

    @section http_client_migration_encodings Compression and Character Encodings

    The connection manager sends an \c Accept-Encoding header with the content codings it decodes (gzip,
    deflate, br, zstd, and bzip2) for requests whose response body it decodes, so servers can compress
    responses; set the \c accept_encoding option to an empty string to send none.  With \c encoding_passthru,
    a compressed body is returned as received.

    @section http_client_migration_proxies Proxies

    A proxy is given with the \c proxy_url option.  HTTPS targets are reached through a \c CONNECT tunnel, and
    the TLS connection through the tunnel is made for the target server: its host name is sent with SNI and
    verified in the server's certificate.  Plain HTTP requests are sent to the proxy with absolute URIs.

    With an \c https proxy URL (an HTTPS proxy), the connection to the proxy uses TLS: the proxy's certificate is
    verified against the proxy's host name, plain HTTP requests are sent to the proxy over TLS, and the TLS
    connection to an HTTPS target is carried by the TLS connection to the proxy.  Other proxy URL schemes raise
    \c HTTPCLIENT-OPTION-ERROR.

    Credentials in the proxy URL are sent with Basic authentication and answer Digest challenges; they are never
    sent through a tunnel.  HTTP/3 is not used through a proxy: in \c "auto" mode HTTP/2 is used instead, and the
    \c "h3" protocol raises an error.

    @section http_client_migration_trailers Request Trailers

    A request body streamed with
    @ref HttpClientIo::HttpClientConnectionManager::acquireAndStartStreaming() "acquireAndStartStreaming()" can
    end with trailer fields, for example a checksum of the body, over HTTP/1.1 (chunked transfer encoding),
    HTTP/2, and HTTP/3: @ref HttpClientIo::HttpClientStreamHandle::setTrailers() "HttpClientStreamHandle::setTrailers()"
    sets them before the call of @ref HttpClientIo::HttpClientStreamHandle::sendData() "sendData()" that ends the
    body.

    @code{.py}
HttpClientStreamHandle sh = mgr.acquireAndStartStreaming("https://upload.example.com", "PUT", "/files/data.bin",
    {"Content-Type": "application/octet-stream"}, True);
sh.setTrailers({"x-checksum": checksum});
sh.sendData(data, True);
    @endcode

    A handler of the @ref httpserverintro "HttpServer" module receives the trailers in \c cx.trailers, and a stream
    handler in the \c hdr key of the last call of \c recv().

    @section http_client_migration_get_body GET Request Bodies

    A GET request body has no defined semantics (RFC 9110 section 9.3.1); @ref Qore::HTTPClient "HTTPClient"
    sends one, and the new modules raise \c HTTPCLIENT-REQUEST-BODY-ERROR unless the \c allow_get_body option
    is set, for APIs that require a body with GET requests.

    @section http_client_migration_errors Exceptions

    |!HTTPClient / RestClient|!HttpClientIo / RestClientIo
    |\c HTTP-CLIENT-RECEIVE-ERROR for an HTTP error status (\c HTTPClient)|none: the response is returned
    |\c HTTP-CLIENT-RECEIVE-ERROR, \c REST-RESPONSE-ERROR, or a server-defined error (\c RestClient)|\c REST-RESPONSE-ERROR with the @ref RestClient::RestResponseInfo "RestResponseInfo" hash as the argument, or the server-defined error of a body with \c err and \c desc keys; \c error_passthru returns the response instead
    |\c SOCKET-TIMEOUT|\c HTTPCLIENT-TIMEOUT
    |\c SOCKET-CONNECT-ERROR, \c SOCKET-SSL-ERROR when connecting|\c HTTPCLIENT-CONNECT-ERROR; the argument has the \c err and \c desc of the transport error; when HTTP/3 and HTTP/2 connections are raced in \c "auto" mode and the TCP connection is refused, \c SOCKET-CONNECT-ERROR
    |\c SOCKET-SEND-ERROR, \c SOCKET-RECV-ERROR, and other transport errors of a request|\c HTTPCLIENT-REQUEST-ERROR
    |\c HTTP-CLIENT-REDIRECT-ERROR, \c HTTP-CLIENT-MAXIMUM-REDIRECTS-EXCEEDED, \c HTTP-CLIENT-UNKNOWN-PROTOCOL|the same
    |\c HTTP-CLIENT-RESPONSE-BODY-TOO-LARGE|the same
    |\c HTTP-CLIENT-URL-ERROR|\c HTTPCLIENT-URL-ERROR

    Two modules that moved to the new transport keep errors of @ref Qore::HTTPClient "HTTPClient" for code
    written against them: the poll operations of the \c ConnectionProvider module raise \c SOCKET-TIMEOUT for a
    timeout and the \c SOCKET-* error of a failed connection, and the data providers of the
    \c HttpClientDataProvider module raise \c SOCKET-TIMEOUT for a timeout and \c HTTP-CLIENT-RECEIVE-ERROR for
    an HTTP error status; other errors are those of the connection manager.

    @section http_client_migration_restclient RestClient to RestClientIo

    |!RestClient|!RestClientIo
    |\c get(), \c post(), \c put(), \c patch(), \c del(), and the inherited \c HTTPClient::head()|\c restGet(), \c restPost(), \c restPut(), \c restPatch(), \c restDel(), \c restHead(), and \c restOptions(), which return a @ref RestClient::RestResponseInfo "RestResponseInfo" hash
    |\c doRequest(), \c doValidatedRequest()|\c restDoRequest(), \c restDoRawRequest()
    |\c doRequestWithResponseLimit()|\c restDoRequestWithResponseLimit()
    |\c startPollDoRequest(), \c startPollGet(), and the other \c startPoll*() methods|\c startPollRequest(); also \c restGetAsync() and the other \c rest*Async() methods, and the SSE APIs
    |\c setTimeout()|\c setTimeout(): the deadline of a response; see @ref http_client_migration_timeouts

    @section http_client_migration_not_supported Differences That Remain

    - HTTP/1.0 requests are not sent; HTTP/1.1 is used instead
    - event and warning queues (\c HTTPClient::setEventQueue(), \c HTTPClient::setWarningQueue()) are not
      supported; use a logger (\c logger option)
    - a connection manager is configured when it is created; the \c set*() methods of
      @ref Qore::HTTPClient "HTTPClient" have no counterpart
    - per-socket statistics are replaced by @ref HttpClientIo::HttpClientConnectionManager::getStats() "getStats()"
      and @ref HttpClientIo::HttpClientConnectionManager::getConnectionInfo() "getConnectionInfo()"
*/
